    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> built the shrine lower than its surroundings and open on all sides, so that worshippers walk down to it, and on 16 August 1604 he placed a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a> in it.</li><li>Mughal and Afghan forces attacked the site repeatedly, most severely in 1762, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> had it blown up with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> returned each time to restore the pool and rebuild.</li><li>In June 1984 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-blue-star">Operation Blue Star</a> against Jarnail Singh Bhindranwale and his followers in the complex damaged the shrine and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a>, and the casualty figures remain disputed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s gilded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> panels for the roof were worth 500,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>, and the first was placed on the shrine in 1803; the gold plating now includes a 400 kilogram dome and 750 kilograms of gold on the upper level.</p></aside>
    </section>

