    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1581</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> began construction of the Harmandir Sahib, keeping the pool empty and dry while it was built</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1604</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> placed a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a> in the gurdwara and appointed Baba Buddha as the first granthi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1762</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> had the Golden Temple blown up with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a> collected donations to rebuild it in 1764</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1830</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> donated gold to overlay the sanctum with gold leaf, after the renovation in marble and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> of 1809</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1905</span><span class="rf-text">Idols and images were removed from the Golden Temple after the campaign of the Tat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1984</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> carried out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-blue-star">Operation Blue Star</a> in the complex between 1 and 8 June, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a> was destroyed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1999</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a> Sahib, after repairs ordered in 1986 were removed</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Golden Temple, also called the Harmandir Sahib or Durbar Sahib, stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. Its early history is hard to reconstruct for lack of records: the few Persian sources tend to be biased, and most of what is known comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> hagiographical literature in the sakhi, mahima, gurbilas and panth prakash genres. These sources were written by poets rather than historians, are reverential toward the site and focus on theology more than on objective history.</p><p>Sikh sources hold that the site had long been associated with religion, fell into obscurity and was rediscovered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-gurus">Sikh gurus</a>. Myths give it older antecedents, among them a Puranic-style account of an amrit kund, a reservoir of nectar, and a legend linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> was healed by amrit brought by his sons Lava and Kush. According to Madanjit Kaur, the low-lying spot was probably a jungle with a pond and a Dukh Bhanjani Beri tree, near a caravan route. Sikh historical records say that Guru Amar Das chose the land, then called Guru Da Chakk, and asked his disciple Ram Das to find a place for a new town with a man-made pool at its centre. Ram Das succeeded him in 1574 and founded the town of Ramdaspur, completing the pool with the help of Baba Buddha and inviting merchants and artisans from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to settle there.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Golden Temple&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Colonial period, Post-independence, Sikh history museums:</p><ul><li>Madanjit Kaur, The Golden Temple: Past and Present (Guru Nanak Dev University Press, 1983)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib.jpg" alt="Guru Granth Sahib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_Guru_Granth_Sahib_Nishan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Golden Temple is a gurdwara, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> house of worship, set in a complex of buildings around the sanctum and the sarovar, the holy pool known as the Amrit Sarovar. The sanctum is a square of 12.25 by 12.25 metres with two storeys and a dome covered in gold leaf. It stands on a marble platform inside a nearly square pool of 154.5 by 148.5 metres and 5.1 metres deep, which a marble passage 3.7 metres wide encircles. Pilgrims usually walk clockwise round the pool before crossing the causeway to the sanctum through the Darshani Deorhi, a two-storey gateway that houses the temple offices and treasury.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>, the Sikh scripture, is seated on the lower floor for about 20 hours each day. Each dawn it is carried in a flower-decorated palanquin across the causeway in the rite called prakash, and each night it returns to its bedroom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a> in the rite called sukhasan. The interior carries mirror work, inlaid stone, embossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and murals, mostly of floral designs. The four entrances to the complex symbolise, in the Sikh view, that people of all groups, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> and ethnicities are welcome. Beside the shrine stand the Akal Takht, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langar-sikhism">langar</a>, which serves a vegetarian meal to all visitors, and museums of Sikh history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akbar.jpg" alt="Akbar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Govardhan._Akbar_With_Lion_and_Calf_ca._1630,_Metmuseum_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Govardhan / Mir Ali Heravi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Guru Ram Das acquired the land, though two accounts exist of how: in one, taken from a Gazetteer record, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> donations of 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> bought it from the people and owners of the village of Tung; in the other, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> donated it to Ram Das's wife. In 1581 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> began construction, keeping the pool empty and dry throughout. The first version took eight years, and the brick gurdwara was complete in 1589. Arjan sited it below the level of the city to stress humility, left the compound open on all sides to show that it was open to all, and gave the sanctum a single bridge. Some later sources say that he invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Mian Mir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> to lay the foundation stone, a belief that is unsubstantiated; traditional Sikh sources such as the Sri Gur Suraj Parkash Granth say that Arjan laid it himself.</p><p>The pool was then filled, and on 16 August 1604 Arjan placed a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a> in the gurdwara. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> in 1802 and announced that he would rebuild the shrine in marble and gold. The first gilded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> panel was placed in 1803, the temple was renovated in marble and copper in 1809, and in 1830 he donated gold to overlay the sanctum. Because so many people contributed, it is difficult to say when parts of the temple were built or decorated, or by whom.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Golden Temple&#8221; cites where it covers Construction, Mughal Empire era destruction and rebuilding, Ranjit Singh era reconstruction:</p><ul><li>Rishi Singh, State Formation and the Establishment of Non-Muslim Hegemony: Post-Mughal 19th-century Punjab (Sage Publications India, 2015)</li><li>Madanjit Kaur, The Golden Temple: Past and Present (Dept. of Guru Nanak Studies, Guru Nanak Dev University Press, 1983)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh.jpg" alt="Ranjit Singh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaj_Ranjit_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colonel James Skinner (1778-1841), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The completion of the first version was, in Arvind-Pal Singh Mandair's words, a milestone for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, since it gave the community a central pilgrimage place and rallying point set within a hub of trade. The canopy on the parkarma called Ath Sath Tirath, the shrine of 68 pilgrimages, reflects the belief that a visit to the temple equals visits to 68 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage sites. Bathing in the pool is believed by many Sikhs to have restorative powers. Sikhism officially admonishes pilgrimage, but an exception has been made for the Golden Temple, owing to the constant singing of hymns there. The Mughal rulers and Afghan sultans viewed it as the centre of the Sikh faith, and so as the main target of persecution.</p><p>Observers disagreed about its style. James Fergusson saw one of the forms into which Hindu temple architecture developed in the 19th century. A colonial list of 1875 claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s design was based on the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Mian Mir. Henry Hardy Cole took it to draw mainly on Islamic sources with significant Hindu input, and Percy Brown called it a synthesis of Islamic and Hindu styles with characteristics of its own. Hermann Goetz admired it but criticised its gaudy elements, and Ian Kerr and other scholars describe a mixture of Indo-Islamic Mughal and Hindu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> architecture. Louis E. Fenech finds the museum display partly ahistorical and typical of a presentation of Sikh history as persecution, martyrdom and bravery in war.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Golden Temple&#8221; cites where it covers Singh Sabha and Akali movements, Jallianwala Bagh massacre, Operation Blue Star:</p><ul><li>Harjot Oberoi, The Construction of Religious Boundaries: Culture, Identity, and Diversity in the Sikh Tradition (University of Chicago Press, 1994)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-arjan.jpg" alt="Guru Arjan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_Arjan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Family workshop of Purkhu of Kangra, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a>, who refused to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and executed him in 1606. In 1709 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> defied an army sent to stop their festival gatherings, and in 1737 the Mughal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> had the custodian Mani Singh executed; Masse Khan, appointed to police the temple, occupied it and befouled the pool, and Sikhs assassinated him there in August 1740. In 1746 Lakhpat Rai had the pool filled with sand, and the Sikhs restored it in 1749. In 1757 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> desecrated the temple, and in 1762 he had it blown up with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>; Sikhs celebrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a> in its ruins. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a> collected donations for its rebuilding in 1764, and on 1 December 1764 Baba Gurbaksh Singh and 29 other Sikhs were killed in a last stand against Afghan forces.</p><p>A new gateway, causeway and sanctum were completed in 1776 and the floor around the pool in 1784. The British built a Gothic clock tower beside the complex between 1862 and 1874, which the Sikhs disliked and demolished in the 1940s. No surviving account or depiction of the shrine before 1764 is known. Most of its murals were lost under later marble panels and in the demolition of the bungas, the buildings that surrounded it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akal-takht.jpg" alt="Akal Takht" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AkalTakhtGoldenTempleComplex.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paasikivi, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Golden Temple became a centre of the Singh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabha-libya">Sabha</a> movement, which removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests, idols and images from it in 1905. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a> of 1919, in which 379 were killed, protests pressed the colonial government to transfer the temple's management to the elected Shiromani Gurdwara Parbandhak Committee, which still manages it. The complex was also the main centre of the Punjabi Suba movement, which led to the present borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1966.</p><p>In the early 1980s Jarnail Singh Bhindranwale and his followers took up residence in the complex. The government claimed that they made it an armoury and headquarters. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> ordered the army to remove them, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-blue-star">Operation Blue Star</a> ran from 1 to 8 June 1984. Official estimates gave 83 army dead and 249 injured, 1,592 militants apprehended and 493 combined militant and civilian casualties, and the government attributed high civilian losses to militants using trapped pilgrims as human shields. The Associated Press correspondent Brahma Chellaney reported about 780 militants and civilians and 400 troops dead, and the government disputed his figures and charged him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> worldwide criticised the operation as an assault on their religion. On 31 October 1984 Gandhi was assassinated by two Sikh bodyguards, and more than 3,000 Sikhs were killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in the riots that followed. Volunteers renovated and drained the pool, and Sikhs rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a> in 1999.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Golden Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> in 1581 and gilded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, is the holiest site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>. Mughal and Afghan armies destroyed it more than once and Sikhs rebuilt it; in 1984 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-blue-star">Operation Blue Star</a> damaged it and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a>.</em></p>

